mc(4, 'matching', `Match the organism with its use and choose the INCORRECT combination.`, `<i>Methanobacterium</i> : produces ethanol in wine making`, [`<i>Methanobacterium</i> : produces methane in biogas`, `<i>Propionibacterium sharmanii</i> : makes holes in Swiss cheese`, `<i>Monascus purpureus</i> : source of statins`], `Ethanol in wine is made by yeast (<i>Saccharomyces</i>). <i>Methanobacterium</i> makes methane.`);
mc(4, 'matching', `All of the following organisms make products that are used in medicine EXCEPT`, `<i>Acetobacter aceti</i>`, [`<i>Trichoderma polysporum</i>`, `<i>Streptococcus</i>`, `<i>Monascus purpureus</i>`], `<i>Acetobacter</i> gives acetic acid (vinegar). The others give cyclosporin A, streptokinase and statins.`);
mc(4, 'matching', `Which of the following sets contains only fungi?`, `<i>Penicillium notatum</i>, <i>Trichoderma polysporum</i>, <i>Aspergillus niger</i>`, [`<i>Aspergillus niger</i>, <i>Acetobacter aceti</i>, <i>Monascus purpureus</i>`, `<i>Penicillium</i>, <i>Clostridium</i>, <i>Glomus</i>`, `<i>Glomus</i>, <i>Lactobacillus</i>, <i>Trichoderma</i>`], `<i>Acetobacter</i>, <i>Clostridium</i> and <i>Lactobacillus</i> are bacteria. The first set has three fungi.`);
mc(4, 'matching', `Which set contains only bacteria?`, `<i>Lactobacillus</i>, <i>Streptococcus</i>, <i>Rhizobium</i>`, [`<i>Lactobacillus</i>, <i>Trichoderma</i>, <i>Rhizobium</i>`, `<i>Anabaena</i>, <i>Glomus</i>, <i>Azotobacter</i>`, `<i>Saccharomyces</i>, <i>Streptococcus</i>, <i>Azotobacter</i>`], `<i>Trichoderma</i>, <i>Glomus</i> and <i>Saccharomyces</i> are fungi. Cyanobacteria are also bacteria in the wider sense, but the third set has a fungus.`, `Cyanobacteria are bacteria, so the key is the fungus in each wrong set.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `Which of the following statements about statins is correct?`, `They act as competitive inhibitors of the enzyme that makes cholesterol`, [`They are produced by <i>Trichoderma polysporum</i>`, `They are antibiotics used for wound healing`, `They dissolve blood clots`], `Statins from the yeast <i>Monascus purpureus</i> compete for the active site of the cholesterol-synthesising enzyme (HMG-CoA reductase). Clot busting is streptokinase.`, `Competitive inhibition is a Class 11 biomolecules idea that joins this chapter.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `A competitive inhibitor like a statin`, `resembles the substrate and competes for the active site`, [`binds far from the active site and changes its shape`, `destroys the enzyme`, `adds to the substrate`], `It is similar in shape to the substrate. The effect can be reduced by adding more substrate.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `The principal reason that cyclosporin A is used for organ transplants is that it`, `suppresses the immune response that causes rejection`, [`kills the bacteria in the donor organ`, `lowers the blood sugar`, `dissolves clots in vessels`], `The recipient's immune system sees the new organ as foreign. Cyclosporin A reduces this response.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `Which of the following is TRUE about streptokinase?`, `It is a protein (an enzyme) that helps remove clots, and genetically engineered bacteria are also used to produce it`, [`It is a lipid from <i>Monascus</i>`, `It is used as a detergent`, `It is a hormone from <i>Aspergillus</i>`], `Streptokinase is an enzyme used as a clot buster. It was first from <i>Streptococcus</i>.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `Yeast in bread dough releases CO<sub>2</sub> by`, `anaerobic fermentation of sugars`, [`photosynthesis`, `aerobic oxidation of fats`, `nitrogen fixation`], `Yeast breaks glucose into ethanol and CO<sub>2</sub> without oxygen (fermentation). The ethanol evaporates when the bread is baked.`, `Respiration chapter link.`);
mc(4, 'industrial', `Which of the following is true about fermentors?`, `They are large vessels where microbes are grown under controlled conditions to get products in bulk`, [`They are small test-tubes used for staining`, `They are used only for distillation`, `They are used only for sewage`], `Industrial fermentors control temperature, pH, aeration and nutrients.`);
mc(4, 'sewage', `Which of the following does NOT happen in the aeration tank?`, `Anaerobic bacteria produce methane`, [`Aerobic microbes consume organic matter`, `Flocs are formed`, `BOD of the effluent falls`], `Methane forms in the anaerobic sludge digester, not in the aerated tank.`);
mc(4, 'sewage', `Which statement about flocs is CORRECT?`, `They are bacteria with fungal filaments forming a mesh that settles as activated sludge`, [`They are floating debris removed in primary treatment`, `They are crystals of mineral salts`, `They form in anaerobic digesters`], `Flocs form in the aerated tank and then settle as activated sludge.`);
mc(4, 'sewage', `If the BOD of effluent leaving a plant is still very high, it will`, `use up much of the dissolved oxygen of the river and harm fish`, [`add oxygen to the river`, `kill all bacteria in the river`, `make water clear`], `High BOD means high demand on dissolved oxygen, so aquatic animals may suffocate.`);
mc(4, 'biogas', `Methanogens are classified as`, `archaebacteria (archaea)`, [`eubacteria that are photosynthetic`, `fungi`, `protozoa`], `Methanogens live in anaerobic places such as the rumen and marshes, and are placed with archaebacteria (Class 11, Kingdom Monera).`, `Mixed with the classification chapter.`);
mc(4, 'biogas', `Which of the following helps to explain why methanogens are present in cattle dung?`, `The rumen of the cattle is anaerobic and the cellulose-rich food is digested there by these microbes`, [`Cattle breathe in methane`, `Cattle eat methanogens along with grass`, `Cattle dung is rich in oxygen`], `The microbes come from the rumen. They are anaerobes and grow well in dung slurry.`);
mc(4, 'biogas', `A biogas plant stops producing gas after a heavy dose of antibiotics is added to the slurry. The most likely reason is that`, `the anaerobic microbes in the slurry were killed or inhibited`, [`methane dissolves in antibiotics`, `oxygen is released`, `the cover has risen too high`], `The microbes that digest the dung are the source of gas. Killing them halts methane production.`);
mc(4, 'biocontrol', `Bt cotton and spraying Bt spores are alike in that`, `both use the toxin of <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i> against insect pests`, [`both use the toxin of a virus`, `both use a ladybird`, `both fix atmospheric nitrogen`], `In Bt cotton the toxin gene is built into the plant (biotechnology chapter). In sprays, the dried spores themselves supply the toxin. Both use Bt toxin.`, `Mixed with the biotechnology chapter.`);
mc(4, 'biocontrol', `Integrated pest management aims to`, `reduce pests through several methods, including biological agents, and cut down on chemicals`, [`apply only chemical sprays`, `kill all insects`, `remove all microbes from the soil`], `IPM combines biological, cultural and limited chemical methods and conserves useful insects.`);
mc(4, 'biocontrol', `Which of the following is the best example of a biocontrol agent that is a fungus?`, `<i>Trichoderma</i>`, [`Baculovirus`, `Ladybird`, `Bt`], `<i>Trichoderma</i> is a fungus. Bt is a bacterium, baculovirus a virus, and ladybird a beetle.`);
mc(4, 'biofertiliser', `Nitrogenase in <i>Rhizobium</i> nodules is protected from oxygen by`, `leghaemoglobin`, [`chlorophyll`, `haemoglobin of human blood`, `cytochrome c`], `Leghaemoglobin in the nodules binds oxygen, giving the anaerobic environment that nitrogenase needs. (Class 11, mineral nutrition.)`, `Mixed with nitrogen metabolism.`);
mc(4, 'biofertiliser', `Which structure helps <i>Anabaena</i> and <i>Nostoc</i> fix nitrogen?`, `Heterocyst`, [`Mesosome`, `Pili`, `Flagellum`], `Heterocysts are thick-walled cells where nitrogen fixation occurs away from the oxygen of photosynthesis (Class 11, Kingdom Monera).`, `Mixed with classification chapter.`);
mc(4, 'biofertiliser', `Which pair correctly shows the type of nitrogen-fixation?`, `<i>Rhizobium</i> : symbiotic; <i>Azotobacter</i> : free-living`, [`<i>Rhizobium</i> : free-living; <i>Azotobacter</i> : symbiotic`, `<i>Glomus</i> : symbiotic N-fixer; <i>Anabaena</i> : free-living fungus`, `<i>Azospirillum</i> : symbiotic; <i>Nostoc</i> : fungus`], `<i>Rhizobium</i> forms nodules with legumes, while <i>Azotobacter</i> and <i>Azospirillum</i> are free-living.`);
mc(4, 'biofertiliser', `Mycorrhiza may increase a plant's tolerance to`, `salinity and drought`, [`cold only`, `X-rays`, `fire`], `It also gives resistance to root-borne pathogens and increases growth.`);
mc(4, 'household', `LAB present in curd and the bacteria of idli batter are similar in that`, `both are bacteria acting by fermentation`, [`both are fungi`, `both need light`, `both make antibiotics`], `Both are bacterial fermentations. Idli batter produces CO<sub>2</sub>, and curd gives lactic acid.`);
mc(4, 'household', `A sample of milk is boiled and cooled to 10 °C before adding curd, but the milk does not set well. The likely reason is that`, `LAB grow slowly at such a low temperature`, [`boiling killed the milk proteins permanently`, `curd contains yeast`, `lactic acid cannot form`], `Microbes need suitable warmth to multiply. In cold weather, curd sets slowly or does not set.`);

asr(4, 'industrial', `Streptokinase is called a "clot buster".`, `It is an enzyme that dissolves clots in blood vessels.`, 0, `It is used in heart-attack patients. R states the action and explains A.`);
asr(4, 'sewage', `Sewage treatment plants help to protect river water.`, `Treated effluent has a lower BOD than raw sewage.`, 0, `A lower BOD means less oxygen consumption in the river. R explains A.`);
asr(4, 'biofertiliser', `Biofertilisers are better than chemical fertilisers for sustainable farming.`, `Biofertilisers are living organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil without chemical residues.`, 0, `They are renewable and pollute less. R explains A.`);

mc(4, 'industrial', `Consider: (i) Citric acid: <i>Aspergillus niger</i>; (ii) Lactic acid: <i>Lactobacillus</i>; (iii) Butyric acid: <i>Acetobacter aceti</i>; (iv) Statins: <i>Monascus purpureus</i>. How many pairs are correct?`, `Three`, [`One`, `Two`, `Four`], `(i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. (iii) is wrong because butyric acid is from <i>Clostridium butylicum</i>; <i>Acetobacter</i> gives acetic acid.`, `One wrong pair among four hides easily.`, 'statements');
mc(4, 'biocontrol', `Consider: (i) Bt is a bacterium; (ii) <i>Trichoderma</i> is a virus; (iii) Baculoviruses are species-specific; (iv) Ladybirds eat aphids. How many statements are correct?`, `Three`, [`Two`, `Four`, `One`], `(ii) is wrong because <i>Trichoderma</i> is a fungus. The other three are correct.`, undefined, 'statements');
asr(4, 'biogas', `A biogas plant works only when air is excluded from the digester.`, `Methanogens are anaerobic microbes.`, 0, `Oxygen would stop methanogens, so air is kept out. R explains A.`);
asr(4, 'industrial', `Brandy has a higher alcohol content than wine.`, `Brandy is produced by distilling the fermented fruit juice, whereas wine is not distilled.`, 0, `Distillation concentrates the alcohol, so R explains A.`);
asr(4, 'biofertiliser', `Mycorrhizal association is a mutualism.`, `The fungus gets sugars from the plant and supplies phosphorus to it.`, 0, `Both partners benefit. R explains A.`);
stm(4, 'biogas', `Methanogens are found in the rumen of cattle.`, `Methanogens need oxygen for growth.`, 2, `They are strict anaerobes, so Statement II is incorrect.`);
stm(4, 'biocontrol', `Ladybirds are used against aphids and dragonflies against mosquitoes.`, `Both ladybirds and dragonflies are insects.`, 0, `Both statements are correct.`);
stm(4, 'sewage', `Primary sludge settles in the primary settling tank.`, `Activated sludge forms in the primary settling tank.`, 2, `Activated sludge forms in the secondary settling tank after aeration. Statement II is incorrect.`);
match(4, 'industrial', 'mw-m6', 'Enzymes and bioactive molecules with uses', [`A. Lipase`, `B. Pectinase and protease`, `C. Streptokinase`, `D. Cyclosporin A`], [`p. Immunosuppressant`, `q. Removal of oily stains`, `r. Clot buster`, `s. Clarifying bottled juices`], `A-q, B-s, C-r, D-p`, [`A-s, B-q, C-r, D-p`, `A-q, B-s, C-p, D-r`, `A-r, B-s, C-q, D-p`], `Lipase works on oils in detergents (q). Pectinase and protease clear juices (s). Streptokinase dissolves clots (r). Cyclosporin A suppresses the immune response (p).`);
match(4, 'sewage', 'mw-m7', 'Sewage treatment terms', [`A. Primary sludge`, `B. Flocs`, `C. Activated sludge`, `D. Anaerobic sludge digester`], [`p. Sediment from the aeration tank, partly returned as inoculum`, `q. Biogas is formed`, `r. Settled solids from the first tank`, `s. Bacteria with fungal filaments`], `A-r, B-s, C-p, D-q`, [`A-s, B-r, C-p, D-q`, `A-r, B-s, C-q, D-p`, `A-p, B-s, C-r, D-q`], `Primary sludge: solids settled first (r). Flocs: bacteria with fungal filaments (s). Activated sludge: sediment of flocs (p). Digester: biogas formed (q).`);

nat(4, 'sewage', `Raw sewage has BOD 360 mg/L. Primary treatment removes about 30% of the BOD, and secondary treatment then removes 90% of what remains. What is the BOD in mg/L of the final effluent?`, 25.2, `After primary: 360 × 0.70 = 252. After secondary: 252 × 0.10 = 25.2 mg/L.`, 'mg/L', `Subtracting 30% and 90% from 360 directly would give a negative number.`, 0.05);
nat(4, 'sewage', `A river receives 2000 L of effluent per hour with BOD 20 mg/L. How many grams of oxygen are needed by microbes in one hour to oxidise the organic matter?`, 40, `Total = 2000 L × 20 mg/L = 40,000 mg = 40 g.`, 'g', `Convert mg to g.`);
nat(4, 'industrial', `How many of the following are enzymes: lipase, pectinase, protease, streptokinase, cyclosporin A, penicillin, statin?`, 4, `Lipase, pectinase, protease and streptokinase are enzymes (4). Cyclosporin A, penicillin and statin are not.`);
nat(4, 'biocontrol', `Among Bt, <i>Trichoderma</i>, baculovirus, ladybird, dragonfly, how many are insects?`, 2, `Ladybird and dragonfly are insects (2). Bt is a bacterium, <i>Trichoderma</i> a fungus and baculovirus a virus.`);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- Level 5
mc(5, 'industrial', `Which of the following is correctly matched with a source that is NOT a fungus?`, `Streptokinase : bacterium`, [`Cyclosporin A : <i>Trichoderma</i>`, `Statin : <i>Monascus</i>`, `Citric acid : <i>Aspergillus</i>`], `Streptokinase is from <i>Streptococcus</i>, a bacterium. The other sources are fungi (a mould, a yeast, a mould).`, `The question asks for the one whose source is not a fungus.`);
mc(5, 'industrial', `Streptomycin, an antibiotic against tuberculosis, is obtained from`, `<i>Streptomyces griseus</i>, a bacterium`, [`<i>Penicillium notatum</i>`, `<i>Cephalosporium</i>`, `<i>Aspergillus niger</i>`], `Many antibiotics come from the soil bacteria called actinomycetes, such as <i>Streptomyces</i>. (Beyond NCERT's text, but an exam favourite.)`, `NCERT names only penicillin; do not assume all antibiotics are from <i>Penicillium</i>.`);
mc(5, 'industrial', `Which of the following is a fungal antibiotic source other than <i>Penicillium</i>?`, `<i>Cephalosporium acremonium</i>`, [`<i>Streptomyces</i>`, `<i>Lactobacillus</i>`, `<i>Rhizobium</i>`], `Cephalosporins come from the fungus <i>Cephalosporium</i>. (Beyond NCERT, a standard supporting fact.)`);
mc(5, 'industrial', `Which of these is NOT used to make a household or industrial product in NCERT?`, `<i>Rhizobium</i> for making curd`, [`<i>Propionibacterium sharmanii</i> for Swiss cheese`, `<i>Acetobacter aceti</i> for vinegar`, `<i>Lactobacillus</i> for lactic acid`], `<i>Rhizobium</i> is a nitrogen-fixer with legumes; it has no role in curd.`);
mc(5, 'industrial', `Pectinases and proteases are added to fruit juice mainly to`, `break down pectin and proteins so the juice becomes clear`, [`kill all bacteria`, `add sugar`, `colour the juice`], `Pectin and protein make the juice cloudy and thick. The enzymes degrade them.`);
mc(5, 'sewage', `In a plant, the activated sludge is NOT sent to the anaerobic digester entirely because`, `a part is needed as an inoculum for the next batch in the aeration tank`, [`it would release oxygen in the digester`, `it would add grit to the river`, `it destroys the flocs permanently`], `The recycled portion keeps a rich population of useful microbes in the aeration tank.`);
mc(5, 'sewage', `Which of these statements about BOD is NOT correct?`, `It measures the amount of CO<sub>2</sub> released in a sample`, [`It measures the oxygen consumed if organic matter in one litre of water is oxidised by bacteria`, `A higher BOD means higher polluting potential`, `It falls after secondary treatment`], `BOD measures oxygen consumed, not CO<sub>2</sub> released.`);
mc(5, 'sewage', `Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan were launched by`, `the Ministry of Environment and Forests, to build sewage treatment plants and save the rivers`, [`the Ministry of Agriculture to grow biofertilisers`, `the Ministry of Health to make vaccines`, `ICAR to make biogas plants`], `These plans aim to reduce river pollution by treating sewage before release.`);
mc(5, 'biogas', `Methanogens convert the products of earlier fermentation into methane. Which of the following would you therefore expect in a biogas digester?`, `A mixture of microbes in which methanogens are the last group acting on the organic matter`, [`Only methanogens acting on raw dung directly`, `Only aerobic bacteria`, `Algae with light`], `Anaerobic digestion has several groups of microbes. Methanogens finish the process by making methane. (An extension of the NCERT idea.)`);
mc(5, 'biogas', `Which pair of gases is CORRECT for biogas formed in a sludge digester according to NCERT?`, `Methane and hydrogen sulphide, with CO<sub>2</sub>`, [`Oxygen and chlorine`, `Nitrogen and ozone`, `Ammonia and hydrogen only`], `NCERT says the gases are methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide.`);
mc(5, 'biocontrol', `Which of the following is the most species-specific biocontrol agent?`, `Baculovirus`, [`Ladybird`, `Dragonfly`, `Chemical insecticide`], `Baculoviruses have a narrow host range, so they do not harm non-target insects. Predators like ladybirds feed on many insects.`);
mc(5, 'biocontrol', `A Bt spore preparation is safe for beneficial insects like honeybees because`, `the spores kill only the larvae of certain insects that eat them and release the toxin in their gut`, [`the spores are not living`, `Bt produces no toxin`, `honeybees are plants`], `Bt toxin acts in the gut conditions of target larvae. NCERT says the spores are harmless to other insects.`);
mc(5, 'biofertiliser', `How many of the following are involved in nitrogen fixation? <i>Rhizobium</i>, <i>Azotobacter</i>, <i>Glomus</i>, <i>Nostoc</i>, <i>Azospirillum</i>`, `Four`, [`Five`, `Three`, `Two`], `<i>Glomus</i> is the only one that does not fix nitrogen; it helps in phosphorus uptake.`, undefined, 'statements');
mc(5, 'biofertiliser', `Which pair of biofertilisers is best for a flooded paddy field and a pulse field respectively?`, `Cyanobacteria and <i>Rhizobium</i>`, [`<i>Rhizobium</i> and cyanobacteria`, `<i>Glomus</i> and <i>Trichoderma</i>`, `Bt and <i>Azotobacter</i>`], `Cyanobacteria float or live in flooded fields. <i>Rhizobium</i> forms nodules on legumes.`);
mc(5, 'biofertiliser', `Which is a symbiotic association of a fungus with the root of a plant that benefits the plant mainly by absorbing phosphorus?`, `<i>Glomus</i> mycorrhiza`, [`<i>Rhizobium</i> nodules`, `<i>Anabaena</i> in <i>Azolla</i>`, `<i>Trichoderma</i> in soil`], `<i>Glomus</i> is the main example. <i>Anabaena</i> in <i>Azolla</i> fixes nitrogen.`);
mc(5, 'household', `Which of these microbes is used for TWO different products in NCERT's account?`, `<i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i>: bread and ethanol`, [`<i>Lactobacillus</i>: Swiss cheese and Roquefort`, `<i>Trichoderma</i>: biogas and curd`, `<i>Penicillium notatum</i>: statins and citric acid`], `Baker's or brewer's yeast is used both in bread making and for ethanol production in wine and beer. The other pairs are wrong.`);

asr(5, 'biofertiliser', `<i>Azotobacter</i> can be used for non-leguminous crops.`, `<i>Azotobacter</i> is a free-living bacterium that fixes nitrogen without needing nodules.`, 0, `Since it does not need a legume host, it can enrich soils for any crop. R explains A.`);
asr(5, 'sewage', `The anaerobic sludge digester does not need an air pump.`, `Anaerobic bacteria in the digester digest the sludge to give biogas.`, 0, `The microbes are anaerobic, so oxygen is excluded. R explains A.`);
asr(5, 'biocontrol', `Baculoviruses are suitable for use where beneficial insects must be conserved.`, `They are species-specific and have no effect on non-target insects.`, 0, `The narrow spectrum makes them safe for non-target organisms. R explains A.`);
stm(5, 'industrial', `Alexander Fleming, Ernest Chain and Howard Florey received the Nobel Prize in 1945.`, `Penicillin was used widely during World War II to treat wounded soldiers.`, 0, `Both statements are correct.`);
stm(5, 'biogas', `IARI and KVIC developed the technology for biogas in India.`, `Gobar gas is produced by the aerobic breakdown of dung.`, 2, `The breakdown is anaerobic. Statement II is incorrect.`);
stm(5, 'biofertiliser', `Anabaena, Nostoc and Oscillatoria are cyanobacteria used as biofertilisers.`, `Cyanobacteria do not add organic matter to the soil.`, 2, `They fix nitrogen and also add organic matter. Statement II is incorrect.`);

nat(5, 'sewage', `A sewage plant processes 5000 L of raw sewage per hour with BOD 280 mg/L. After treatment the BOD is 14 mg/L. How many grams of oxygen demand per hour has the plant removed?`, 1330, `Demand removed per litre = 280 - 14 = 266 mg/L. For 5000 L: 266 × 5000 = 1,330,000 mg = 1330 g per hour.`, 'g', `Convert mg to g, and use the difference of BOD values.`);
nat(5, 'sewage', `If the percentage BOD removal in a plant is 95%, and the raw sewage has BOD 400 mg/L, what is the BOD of the effluent in mg/L?`, 20, `Remaining fraction = 5%. So 400 × 0.05 = 20 mg/L.`, 'mg/L', `Do not report the removed amount (380) as the answer.`);
nat(5, 'industrial', `How many of these processes involve distillation in the production of the drink: wine, beer, whisky, brandy, rum, toddy?`, 3, `Whisky, brandy and rum are distilled. Wine, beer and toddy are not. So 3.`);
nat(5, 'matching', `Count the pairs that are correct: (i) <i>Lactobacillus</i>: curd, (ii) <i>Methanobacterium</i>: biogas, (iii) <i>Monascus</i>: cyclosporin A, (iv) <i>Glomus</i>: nitrogen fixation, (v) <i>Clostridium butylicum</i>: butyric acid.`, 3, `(i), (ii) and (v) are correct. <i>Monascus</i> gives statins, not cyclosporin A; <i>Glomus</i> helps in phosphorus uptake, not nitrogen fixation. So 3.`);


